[1m[38;2;255;169;64marmada guild — you, portable: machine-global, and synced between your machines[0m

[1m[38;2;255;169;64mVERBS[0m
  init             [38;2;156;163;175mbuild your guild by interviewing you[0m
  project          [38;2;156;163;175mput your guild where Claude Code will read it[0m
  pull             [38;2;156;163;175mbring this machine's guild up to date[0m
  push             [38;2;156;163;175msend local guild changes to the remote[0m
  export           [38;2;156;163;175mwrite the whole guild to one portable file[0m
  import <bundle>  [38;2;156;163;175mrestore a guild from a bundle[0m
  browse           [38;2;156;163;175msee what is in your guild, and open it[0m
  edit <item>      [38;2;156;163;175mopen one guild file, validate it, and commit it[0m
  delete <item>    [38;2;156;163;175mremove one thing from your guild, and commit the removal[0m

[1m[38;2;255;169;64mNOT BUILT YET[0m
  verify  [38;2;156;163;175mcross-check every workflow, skill and scope[0m

`armada guild <verb> --help` for one verb's usage and flags.

Your guild is how you work: your voice, your skills, your workflows. Set it up
once and it follows you — a new laptop is a `pull` rather than an afternoon.

It never lands in a project's history, and what is only true of this machine
stays on it: its settings, its database, and the Jobs running here.
